  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Central Entity denotes a broader or global reference point within the system that may cause confusion for users choosing entities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Central Entity denotes the primary location or organizational unit within OneStream, where access is permitted for report presentation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Central Entity denotes the primary organizational structure that governs or oversees local entities, serving as a point of reference for users."&lt;SEP&gt;"Central entity is a main location in the OneStream system that provides access to the financial reports being referenced."&lt;SEP&gt;"Central entity refers to the overarching or global entity that the system defaults to when user selections are incorrect."&lt;SEP&gt;"The central entity referred to is the main organizational unit within OneStream under which the reports are typically accessed."&lt;SEP&gt;"The central entity refers to the main organization where permissions to access the P&amp;L MPC report are determined."&lt;SEP&gt;"Central Entity refers to the main organizational unit within OneStream that allows broader access to reports, contrasting with local entities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Central entity represents the main organization or overarching system entity, in contrast to local entities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Central Entity refers to a broader data selection point within the system, contrasted against local entities."&lt;SEP&gt;"The central entity refers to the primary organizational scope required for the report access, which the user is not based in."&lt;SEP&gt;"Central Entity refers to the main organizational framework in OneStream where users typically should be positioned for access rights."&lt;SEP&gt;"The central entity refers to a main or global organization within a system that contrasts with local entities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Central entity refers to the main organization that hosts the data, contrasting with local entities like EPG_Legal that affect user permissions."&lt;SEP&gt;"The central entity refers to the main organization or structure that oversees or coordinates various local entities."&lt;SEP&gt;"The central entity refers to an overarching organization that differs from localized entities in a selection process."&lt;SEP&gt;"The central entity refers to the main organization or hub from which data access is typically managed, in contrast to local entities."&lt;SEP&gt;"The central entity refers to the main organization base where access permissions are typically granted, contrasting with the local entity."&lt;SEP&gt;"The central entity refers to the primary organization which users can select from in the entity selection process."&lt;SEP&gt;"Central entity refers to the main organizational structure or authority, serving as a point of reference for comparison to local entities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Central entity refers to the primary location or organization point in the OneStream system, which holds data accessible to users with proper permissions."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Central Entity represents the core organization structure in which the user is not based, affecting report accessibility."&lt;SEP&gt;"The central entity refers to the overarching or global entity that may be incorrectly selected instead of a user's local entity in the dropdown menu."&lt;SEP&gt;"Central Entity refers to the overarching organization or entity that is contrasted with local entities in the context."&lt;SEP&gt;"Central Entity refers to the main organization or governing body which contrasts with local entities in the selection process."&lt;SEP&gt;"The central entity refers to a primary location or aggregation point in the system where users interact with data."&lt;SEP&gt;"The central entity refers to the main or primary location within the organizational structure where the report presentation is typically managed."&lt;SEP&gt;"Central entity refers to a primary or overarching designation within the organizational structure that typically has broader access or permissions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Central entity represents a broader or global configuration within the system, contrasting with local entities."&lt;SEP&gt;"The 'central entity' is referenced as the main organizational unit in contrast to local entities, suggesting a hierarchical structure."&lt;SEP&gt;"The central entity refers to the main entity within OneStream which users are typically expected to be based in for accessing reports and data."&lt;SEP&gt;"Central Entity refers to the primary location within OneStream where users typically should have access to report presentations."&lt;SEP&gt;"The central entity refers to a primary organizational framework that users can interact with within the system."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Central Entity is referred to as a broader organization within which the local entities operate, influencing the selection process."&lt;SEP&gt;"The central entity is the primary location where data and reporting permissions are managed within OneStream, contrasting with local entities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Central Entity refers to the primary or main entity location relevant for accessing P&amp;L reports within the OneStream framework."&lt;SEP&gt;"The central entity refers to a primary or overarching entity that can be distinguished from local entities in the context of the system."&lt;SEP&gt;"Central Entity represents a broader or overarching location within the system, contrasted with the local entity for user selection."&lt;SEP&gt;"Central entity refers to the primary location or organization that manages data access within the reporting framework of OneStream."&lt;SEP&gt;"The central entity is referenced as a point of access that is not where the relevant user is based for report presentation."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Central Entity refers to a key organization within the system framework that serves as a reference point for users, typically providing overarching management of data and entities."&lt;SEP&gt;"The central entity refers to the main organizational unit within the reporting structure, contrasting with local entities like EPG_Legal."&lt;SEP&gt;"Central Entity signifies a main organization referenced in the context, which users might mistakenly select instead of local entities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Central Entity is the main entity from which the report is supposed to be accessed, contrasting with the local entity of the user."&lt;SEP&gt;"Central entity refers to an overarching organization that users can select within the software system."&lt;SEP&gt;"Central entity refers to a main or global entity in the system that is usually the default selection for users."&lt;SEP&gt;"The central entity represents the main location or system from which data is typically accessed in the OneStream environment, contrasting with local entities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Central Entity refers to the main or global entity within the system that is compared to the user's local entity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Central Entity refers to the principal organization that the user is not based in, leading to the access issue."&lt;SEP&gt;"Central Entity refers to the main location that typically has access to the data but the user is not based there, affecting the report availability."&lt;SEP&gt;"The central entity refers to a primary entity in the system that users are expected to interact with, in contrast to local entities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Central Entity refers to a location or organizational center from which other entities are based, indicating a hierarchical structure in the system."&lt;SEP&gt;"The central entity refers to the primary organizational unit within OneStream where users typically have access to reports, contrasting with the local entity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Central Entity refers to the main organizational structure or site where access to the report should be permitted."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Central Entity refers to the main or global entity that may not correspond to the User's local entity, leading to potential selection issues."&lt;SEP&gt;"Central entities are the main organizational bodies that typically hold broader access 