<node id="&quot;WIRED CONNECTION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Wired Connection refers to a direct physical connection to a network, which can provide more stable internet access than wireless."&lt;SEP&gt;"Wired Connection refers to a direct physical connection to the internet, often more stable than wireless options."&lt;SEP&gt;"Wired connection refers to a method of connecting to the internet using physical cables, often providing a more stable connection than wireless."&lt;SEP&gt;"Wired connection refers to a type of internet connection that uses physical cables to connect devices directly to the network for more stable connectivity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Wired connection refers to a physical data link using cables, which can provide a more stable and faster internet connection compared to wireless options."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-978048478f3c25ba13ac704f6f6775ca</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;HEAVY BANDWIDTH PROGRAMS&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Heavy Bandwidth Programs are applications that use significant network resources, potentially hindering internet performance during use."&lt;SEP&gt;"Heavy bandwidth programs are applications that consume a large amount of internet data, such as video calls and streaming services."&lt;SEP&gt;"Heavy bandwidth programs refer to applications that consume significant network resources, potentially slowing down internet performance for other activities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Heavy bandwidth programs are applications that consume significant network resources, such as video calls or streaming services, which can disrupt other internet activities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Heavy bandwidth programs refer to applications or services that consume significant internet bandwidth, such as video calls or streaming services."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-978048478f3c25ba13ac704f6f6775ca</data>
</node>
