<node id="&quot;COMMON TABLE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A common table is a central database schema used for mapping across multiple regions, emphasizing the importance of coherence in data formatting."&lt;SEP&gt;"A common table is a centralized data structure that serves as a reference for mappings, which workflows rely on."&lt;SEP&gt;"A common table is a universal dataset shared across various applications, serving as a standard reference for mapping data."&lt;SEP&gt;"Common Table refers to a database or data structure that is widely used across workflows to standardize information."&lt;SEP&gt;"Common Tables are central datasets that are intended to be widely used across various functions or regional instances but may differ in data formatting."&lt;SEP&gt;"Common Table denotes a shared database structure that multiple workflows can access for consistency in data."&lt;SEP&gt;"A common table refers to a standardized dataset used across various regions or functions to ensure uniformity in data mapping."&lt;SEP&gt;"Common Table refers to a standardized data structure that is used across multiple regions or departments for uniformity in data processing."&lt;SEP&gt;"A common table is a database structure that holds universal data points utilized across various workflows or applications for consistency and standardization."&lt;SEP&gt;"Common table is a centralized data storage format that is used for mapping across various workflows, making it essential for effective data validation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Common Table refers to a database table that is universally accessible across different workflows, essential for accurate data mapping."&lt;SEP&gt;"Common Table is a standardized database structure that multiple workflows reference, differing from regional-specific mappings."&lt;SEP&gt;"The common table is a database table used universally across systems, serving as a reference point for mapping."&lt;SEP&gt;"Common table refers to a centralized database or reference point that standardizes values across regional tables, facilitating the mapping process."&lt;SEP&gt;"Common Table refers to a universally accessible table within financial systems that standardizes data for easier mapping and validation."&lt;SEP&gt;"A common table is a unified data structure accessible across different workflows, intended to support a broader range of data entries and configurations."&lt;SEP&gt;"Common Table refers to a universally applicable table used in data mapping processes across various regions."&lt;SEP&gt;"A common table is a shared data structure accessible across different systems or departments, used for standardized data values."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7c686046d60abc94ecd48dc9fcb9279e</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;REGIONAL TABLE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A Regional Table refers to a data structure that contains mappings specific to certain regions, which may differ from common tables used in broader workflows."&lt;SEP&gt;"A regional table is a localized dataset that may have unique mapping rules applicable to specific geographical areas."&lt;SEP&gt;"Regional Table indicates a localized version of a database that can contain specific data relevant to a particular region."&lt;SEP&gt;"A regional table is a specific data storage structure that may contain mapping information for a particular region, impacting data workflows."&lt;SEP&gt;"A regional table refers to a specific subset of data formatting rules that can cause discrepancies if not reflected in central mapping rules."&lt;SEP&gt;"Regional Tables are specific datasets that hold mapping values relevant to a particular region, which may not be available in common tables."&lt;SEP&gt;"Regional Table signifies a localized database used for specific areas, possibly containing mappings relevant to a particular region."&lt;SEP&gt;"A regional table is a specific dataset that may contain mapping rules relevant only to particular geographic or functional subsets within a larger database."&lt;SEP&gt;"A regional table is a database classification in which data is stored specifically related to a certain region, which can affect data mapping consistency."&lt;SEP&gt;"Regional table refers to a data storage format that contains mappings specific to a certain region, which might not be universally applicable across all systems."&lt;SEP&gt;"Regional Table pertains to a database table that contains data specific to certain regions, which may not be applicable for all workflows."&lt;SEP&gt;"Regional Table refers to a specific data categorization within the financial systems that may not point to common data but only within a certain region."&lt;SEP&gt;"A regional table is a specific database table that contains values for a certain geographical area, which may impact mapping rules."&lt;SEP&gt;"Regional table refers to a more localized data table that may contain specific mappings for certain regions, which could lead to inconsistencies with common tables."&lt;SEP&gt;"Regional Table indicates a localized version of a data table that may cause discrepancies if not properly integrated with common tables in financial systems."&lt;SEP&gt;"A regional table is a data structure that organizes information pertinent to specific geographic areas or conditions, used for localized data mapping."&lt;SEP&gt;"Regional Table denotes a specific subset of data that is localized for a particular geographic area within the overall system."&lt;SEP&gt;"A regional table refers to a data structure that might store localized values for specific regions within a broader data management context."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7c686046d60abc94ecd48dc9fcb9279e</data>
</node>
