  <data key="d1">"The time of login is a specific timestamp when the user attempted to access the system, which is required information for troubleshooting."&lt;SEP&gt;"Time of Login refers to the specific moment the User attempted to log in, which is relevant information for troubleshooting the error."&lt;SEP&gt;"Time of login refers to the specific moment a user attempts to access a system, important for tracking issues."&lt;SEP&gt;"Time of login refers to the specific moment when the user attempts to access the system, relevant for diagnosing errors."&lt;SEP&gt;"Time of Login refers to the specific moment when the user attempted to access their account, important for troubleshooting."&lt;SEP&gt;"Time of Login refers to the specific moment when a user attempts to access their account, which is relevant for troubleshooting login issues."&lt;SEP&gt;"Time of Login is the specific timestamp when the user attempted to access the account, relevant for troubleshooting the error."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d65f315ad08478c90c34db6e2ef629ae</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;TARGET VALUE MISSING&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Target Value Missing is a specific discrepancy encountered in the OneStream system indicating that a value cannot be matched to a valid member within the database."&lt;SEP&gt;"Target Value Missing is an error message in OneStream indicating a mapping issue in the system."&lt;SEP&gt;"Target value missing is a specific validation error that indicates a failure in mapping a value during a process within OneStream."&lt;SEP&gt;"Target Value Missing is a specific error message indicating that a valid OneStream member could not be identified for mapping."&lt;SEP&gt;"Target Value Missing is an event that occurs during the validation process in OneStream, indicating mapping issues with source files."&lt;SEP&gt;"Target value missing is an event that occurs during OneStream validation when there is an issue mapping a source file value to a valid member."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-5b7a803e592df460426992a1ccbab8b6</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;MAPPING RULES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Mapping Rules, also known as Transformation Rules, are guidelines that ensure data consistency and correctness across different systems when integrating account information."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mapping Rules, also known as Transformation Rules, are guidelines that ensure proper conversion of data values between systems to prevent validation issues."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mapping Rules, also known as Transformation Rules, are processes that ensure all source values are accurately covered and matched across different systems."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mapping rules are a set of guidelines used to ensure that data from various sources is correctly aligned and validated across different systems."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mapping rules are defined guidelines that determine how different data values are transformed and validated across systems to ensure consistency."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mapping rules (or Transformation Rules) define the criteria for aligning data values between different systems to ensure consistency and accuracy."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-7c686046d60abc94ecd48dc9fcb9279e</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;TRANSFORMATION RULES&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Transformation Rules are a component within the Application used for mapping and data processing, involving validation and adjustments of values."&lt;SEP&gt;"Transformation Rules refer to the specific protocols for converting data formats to prevent validation errors and discrepancies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Transformation Rules are mapping rules that ensure consistency and correctness of data between different systems like SAP, Dynamics, and OneStream."&lt;SEP&gt;"Transformation Rules refers to a set of guidelines used to manage how data is transformed within the application, which is essential for ensuring accurate data mapping."&lt;SEP&gt;"Transformation Rules are guidelines for mapping source values to ensure consistency and prevent validation failures in systems like SAP, Dynamics, and OneStream."&lt;SEP&gt;"Transformation Rules are procedures that guide how data values are validated and transformed within the application."&lt;SEP&gt;"Transformation Rules refer to a set of guidelines or procedures that dictate how data should be processed and validated within the Application."&lt;SEP&gt;"Transformation Rules are a set of guidelines used for mapping values and handling validation errors within the Application."&lt;SEP&gt;"Transformation Rules are guidelines that dictate how source values must be mapped and transformed to ensure compatibility between different data systems."&lt;SEP&gt;"Transformation Rules are processes within the Application aimed at addressing issues related to validation errors and data mapping."&lt;SEP&gt;"Transformation Rules are guidelines or protocols within OneStream that dictate how dimensions should be mapped and processed in validation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Transformation Rules are guidelines that ensure that data is consistently structured and formatted across different systems to prevent validation failures."&lt;SEP&gt;"Transformation Rules outline the procedures for handling data validation errors and necessary adjustments in a system."&lt;SEP&gt;"Transformation Rules are defined processes or guidelines used within the Application to ensure data validation and processing."&lt;SEP&gt;"Transformation Rules are the guidelines or protocols that dictate how data values should be mapped from one system to another to ensure consistency and accuracy."&lt;SEP&gt;"Transformation Rules refer to the guidelines or protocols that dictate how certain processes should be transformed or handled in the OneStream system."&lt;SEP&gt;"Transformation Rules refer to the set of guidelines that determine how dimensions should be mapped and transformed during the validation process."&lt;SEP&gt;"Transformation Rules are guidelines that ensure accurate data mapping and validation across different systems, addressing potential discrepancies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Transformation Rules are guidelines within OneStream that govern how data should be mapped and validated."&lt;SEP&gt;"Transformation Rules is an event where specific processes are defined to manage how data values are converted or mapped."&lt;SEP&gt;"Transformation Rules refers to the guidelines or protocols that dictate how data should be transformed or processed within the OneStream System."&lt;SEP&gt;"Transformation Rules are mapping rules designed to cover every possible source value in data integration workflows to prevent mismatches."&lt;SEP&gt;"Transformation Rules are processes within the Application that dictate how data is modified or transformed, essential for ensuring accurate output."&lt;SEP&gt;"Transformation Rules refer to predefined guidelines or procedures that dictate how data dimensions should be mapped and transformed in the validation process."&lt;SEP&gt;"Transformation Rules refer to the guidelines or protocols established to ensure accurate mapping and functioning within the OneStream System."&lt;SEP&gt;"Transformation Rules are guidelines that dictate how dimensions and data mappings should be handled in OneStream."&lt;SEP&gt;"Transformation Rules are processes or guidelines that dictate how data is modified or validated within the Application."&lt;SEP&gt;"Transformation Rules are the mapping rules that must be comprehensive to ensure accuracy across different accounting systems."&lt;SEP&gt;"Transformation Rules refer to guidelines or protocols that dictate how certain processes or mappings should occur within the OneStream system."&lt;SEP&gt;"Transformation Rules are mapping rules that govern how different source values are matched and validated during the data integration process."&lt;SEP&gt;"Transformation Rules is a process or set of guidelines within the Application that deals with resolving mapping issues and errors."&lt;SEP&gt;"Transformation Rules refer to the guidelines and protocols that dictate how systems should be mapped and validated, playing a key role in operational procedures."&lt;SEP&gt;"Transformation Rules are a set of mapping guidelines aimed at ensuring that different source values are consistent across various systems for accurate financial reporting."&lt;SEP&gt;"Transformation Rules are guidelines that dictate how dimensions should be automatically mapped within the data."&lt;SEP&gt;"Transformation Rules are the processes or guidelines used to modify data for validation purposes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Transformation Rules refer to the guidelines or criteria used for mapping data automatically within the OneStream System."&lt;SEP&gt;"Transformation Rules are guidelines that govern how dimensions should be mapped automatically during data processing."&lt;SEP&gt;"Transformation Rules are mapping rules designed to convert, validate, and ensure compatibility among different system data formats in integration processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Transformation Rules are procedures followed to manage data validation errors and ensure correct mapping of values."&lt;SEP&gt;"Transformation Rules refer to the guidelines or protocols that determine how data should be mapped or transformed within the system."&lt;SEP&gt;"Transformation Rules are mapping rules created to cover possible variations in source values for data integration purposes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Transformation Rules are the guidelines or protocols used to modify or correct data values within the Application."&lt;SEP&gt;"Transformation Rules refer to the guidelines or protocols that dictate how certain processes should be executed within the OneStream system."&lt;SEP&gt;"Transformation rules define how data elements are mapped and processed within OneStream, potentially impacting validation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Transformation Rules outline the guidelines for how dimensions should be mapped automatically in the data validation process."&lt;SEP&gt;"Transformation Rules refers to the events or processes governing how data is transformed or validated within the Application."&lt;SEP&gt;"Transformation rules are specific guidelines or criteria that dictate how data or processes should be modified within the system."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-cd1397725188cf9637d6442d0c5e89f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d66999398f94b7cc6674c227a4079c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c686046d60abc94ecd48dc9fcb9279e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6b60420ea1af607508cb6e918ae83d8</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;APPLICATION&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Application appears to be a platform or system referenced for managing processes, likely related to data transformation or workflow."&lt;SEP&gt;"Application refers to a system or platform involved in processing and validating data, where users can access Transformation Rules."&lt;SEP&gt;"Application refers to the system or platform where transformation rules are managed, highlighting its role in the validation process."&lt;SEP&gt;"Application refers to a system or software component involved in processing data and managing validation errors."&lt;SEP&gt;"Application refers to a system or platform that processes data and requires validation for transformation rules."&lt;SEP&gt;"Application refers to a system or platform that involves transformation rules and validation processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Application refers to a system or platform that involves specific transformation rules related to data handling."&lt;SEP&gt;"Application refers to the context within which transformation rules are managed and possibly indicates a software or platform used for handling data processing."&lt;SEP&gt;"Application is a system or tool used for processing data and managing workflows, mentioned in the context of transformation rules."&lt;SEP&gt;"Application refers to a system or platform where transformation rules and validation errors are managed."&lt;SEP&gt;"Application refers to a software or system in which the user can perform specific tasks, including generating transformation rules."&lt;SEP&gt;"Application refers to the software or system involved in the validation process and transformation rules."&lt;SEP&gt;"Application refers to a system or software that is used to manage or process data, highlighting its role in handling validation errors."&lt;SEP&gt;"Application refers to a system or tool used for validation and transformation rules in a data processing context."&lt;SEP&gt;"Application refers to a system or platform where transformation rules are applied and validation errors are managed."&lt;SEP&gt;"Application refers to a software or system that is involved in processing and validating data values, playing a key role in the workflow."&lt;SEP&gt;"Application refers to a system or platform where the validation process is being managed, involving rules and transformations."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-cd1397725188cf9637d6442d0c5e89f0</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;VALIDATION ERROR&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A validation error occurs when data does not meet predefined criteria or rules, indicating a flaw in the data submission or processing."&lt;SEP&gt;"A validation error refers to a failure in confirming that data meets certain criteria within the Application, indicating issues that need to be resolved."&lt;SEP&gt;"A validation error refers to a situation where a source value is missing or does not conform to the expected format requirements."&lt;SEP&gt;"Validation Error describes an issue arising from the inability to process data due to mapping mistakes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Validation Error refers to an issue encountered during the data validation process, indicating that a required value is missing."&lt;SEP&gt;"Validation Error refers to the issue encountered when there is a discrepancy in data mapping, indicating a failure to align data correctly."&lt;SEP&gt;"Validation error refers to an occurrence when the expected data value is missing in the application, indicating a need for correction."&lt;SEP&gt;"Validation error refers to an occurrence in the application where data fails to meet specific criteria, prompting further investigation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Validation Error refers to an issue encountered during a process, indicating that there has been a problem with the mapping or workflow."&lt;SEP&gt;"Validation error refers to an issue that arises when a source value is missing, indicating a problem in the data processing."&lt;SEP&gt;"Validation Error refers to an issue that arises when a specific source value is missing during a data processing activity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Validation Error refers to an issue encountered when data is incorrect or missing, necessitating corrective action within the application."&lt;SEP&gt;"Validation Error refers to an issue that arises during the process of data validation, specifically a mapping error."&lt;SEP&gt;"Validation Error is a failure that occurs during the process of validating data or configurations in a system."&lt;SEP&gt;"Validation error refers to issues or discrepancies found during the data processing that require resolution."&lt;SEP&gt;"Validation Error refers to the overall issue detected during the process when the data does not meet the expected criteria, leading to errors during validation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Validation Error refers to an occurrence that indicates a missing source value, prompting further investigation into data integrity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Validation Error indicates an issue identified during the data processing that requires correction before proceeding."&lt;SEP&gt;"Validation Error refers to an issue that occurs when expected data values are missing or incorrect, leading to interruptions in processing."&lt;SEP&gt;"A validation error refers to a specific issue that occurs when a required source value is missing during data processing."&lt;SEP&gt;"Validation error refers to a specific issue identified during the data processing phase where expected values are missing or incorrect."&lt;SEP&gt;"Validation Error indicates a failure in the process where data does not meet the expected criteria and needs to be addressed."&lt;SEP&gt;"Validation error refers to issues encountered in data processing that require attention and resolution."&lt;SEP&gt;"Validation Error refers to an occurrence indicating that a required source value is missing in the process that needs to be addressed."&lt;SEP&gt;"Validation Error is an event related to the failure of a system process involving mapping and validation, indicating issues that need to be addressed."&lt;SEP&gt;"Validation error refers to an occurrence where data fails to meet specific requirements, prompting a review or adjustment in the application process."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-cd1397725188cf9637d6442d0c5e89f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a18c3e1c2bbe691cf689c00b9522b1d</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;SOURCE VALUE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Source Value denotes the original data input point, which must be accurately mapped across systems to maintain consistency."&lt;SEP&gt;"Source Value is a specific data point that is required but missing, leading to a validation error that must be resolved."&lt;SEP&gt;"Source Value is the specific data element that is lacking and needs to be identified for validation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Source Values refer to the original data points that need proper mapping and transformation when integrated into different financial systems."&lt;SEP&gt;"Source value is the original data point that is being referenced for validation, which may be absent or incorrect."&lt;SEP&gt;"Source value refers to the original data point that is being validated and may be missing or incorrect."&lt;SEP&gt;"Source value represents the specific data point that is identified as missing in the validation process, leading to errors in applications."&lt;SEP&gt;"Source value represents the original input or data point that is being validated in the Application for accuracy and appropriateness."&lt;SEP&gt;"Source value is the specific data point or identifier from the source data that is not recognized by OneStream."&lt;SEP&gt;"Source value refers to the specific data item or value within the source data that is not recognized by OneStream, leading to error messages."&lt;SEP&gt;"Source value refers to the specific data point or piece of information that is lacking or incorrect, which triggers the need for further action."&lt;SEP&gt;"Source value refers to the specific data point or value from the source that is unrecognized in OneStream."&lt;SEP&gt;"Source Value refers to the specific data entry that is identified as missing and requires attention during the mapping process."&lt;SEP&gt;"Source value is a specific item or data point in the source data that may not be recognized by OneStream, prompting an error message."&lt;SEP&gt;"Source Value is the specific value that is missing or incorrect in the validation process, which causes the validation error."&lt;SEP&gt;"Source value denotes the specific data point or value that the system does not recognize, causing an error."&lt;SEP&gt;"Source value is the specific data point or variable that triggers the validation error, indicating missing information."&lt;SEP&gt;"Source value is the original input data that is being evaluated, noted for being missing in the context of validation errors."&lt;SEP&gt;"Source value is the expected data point that should be present in the validation process but is currently missing."&lt;SEP&gt;"Source value indicates the original data point that is missing or problematic within the context of data transformation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Source Value refers to the original data point needing validation and correction within the Application workflows."&lt;SEP&gt;"Source value refers to the data point or item that is missing and needs to be addressed in the validation error process."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-cd1397725188cf9637d6442d0c5e89f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c686046d60abc94ecd48dc9fcb9279e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-974b998f48ab04386086634cf8482ca8</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;MAPPING REQUEST&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A mapping request is a formal procedure initiated when a value is missing or incorrect within the system, detailing the necessary information to correct it."&lt;SEP&gt;"A mapping request is a formal request made to address discrepancies in data, providing details like the value, description, and intended target."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mapping Request is a formal request process initiated when a required value is not found, detailing the value, description, and intended target."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mapping request is a process initiated when a value is missing, seeking information on how to align it with intended targets."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mapping request is a formal request for adjustments regarding data values, descriptions, and their intended targets within the application."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mapping request is the action taken to address the missing value, involving details about the value, description, and intended target."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mapping Request is a formal request submitted to address issues with data mapping, containing details about the problem."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mapping Request is a formal request submitted to address issues with data values, descriptions, and intended targets in the application process."&lt;SEP&gt;"A mapping request is a formal request made to address missing or mismatched source values in data processing."&lt;SEP&gt;"A mapping request is a formal request submitted to resolve discrepancies in data by providing details such as value, description, and intended target."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mapping Request is a process initiated when a source value is missing, requiring details about the value and intended target."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mapping Request involves communicating the details of a missing or incorrect value along with desired corrections for data management."&lt;SEP&gt;"A mapping request is a formal request made when a source value is missing or incorrect, providing details to facilitate correction."&lt;SEP&gt;"A mapping request is an action taken to address discrepancies or missing values in data mapping."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mapping request is a formal inquiry made to correct or establish the mapping of values during data transformation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mapping Request is a formal request to address issues pertaining to value mapping in the Application or Transformation Rules."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mapping request refers to the action taken to address a missing value, including details about the value, description, and intended target."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-cd1397725188cf9637d6442d0c5e89f0</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;RULE PROFILE&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"A Rule Profile determines the guidelines and criteria under which data transformations are processed, indicating different standards for data regions or types."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rule Profile distinguishes between different sets of criteria applied to data based on regional or common standards."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rule Profile is a guideline or set of instructions that influences how workflows are managed, referring to either regional or common standards."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rule Profile refers to a set of guidelines used in workflows to determine how data should be handled based on specified regional or common parameters."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rule Profile refers to configurations used within the workflow, distinguishing between different operational settings like regional vs. common."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rule Profile refers to the specific set of guidelines or criteria within the application that dictates how data should be processed."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rule Profile represents an organizational guideline that defines how workflows should operate, either regionally or commonly."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rule Profile refers to the set of guidelines that determine how data should be transformed or validated within the application, and can vary regionally or commonly."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rule Profile refers to the guidelines that govern how workflows operate, specifically distinguishing between regional and common profiles."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rule Profile refers to the set of rules that govern how data is transformed, which can vary by region or common use."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rule Profile is a set of guidelines or configurations that dictate the behavior of a workflow based on region or commonality."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rule Profile refers to a set of guidelines or criteria that dictates how data should be processed or validated, potentially varying by regional or common standards."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rule Profile refers to the set of criteria or conditions under which the application processes data, differing between regional and common workflows."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rule Profile refers to a configuration setting that determines how data is processed in different regions or contexts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rule Profile represents different configurations or guidelines (regional vs. common) that dictate how data transformation rules are applied."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rule Profile represents the categories or templates that guide how data is structured and interpreted, with distinctions between regional and common templates."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rule Profile distinguishes between different sets of transformation rules, affecting how data is processed based on regional versus common settings."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rule Profile refers to a set of guidelines or criteria that dictate how data is processed or mapped, specifically in a regional or common context."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rule Profile refers to a set of guidelines or configurations that determine how data should be processed, which can vary regionally or commonly."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rule Profile refers to a set of predefined guidelines or standards that govern data mapping and validation in different contexts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rule Profile defines the set of rules used in a specific context (regional or common) for data transformation and validation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rule Profile is a framework or set of guidelines that influences how data is processed in specific contexts, emphasizing regional or common applications."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rule Profile refers to a set of guidelines or criteria that affects the mapping process in workflows."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rule Profile refers to the classification of transformation rules, which can vary by region, affecting how data is processed and validated."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rule Profile signifies a set of guidelines or parameters that dictate how mapping and validation should be executed, varying by region or common standards."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rule Profile describes a specific set of guidelines or parameters that dictate how data mapping and validation should be performed."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rule Profile is a set of guidelines or criteria that dictate how transformation rules are applied, depending on geographic or functional relevance."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rule Profile refers to a specific set of rules which may vary between regional settings and common settings in workflows."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rule Profile refers to the established guidelines that dictate how workflows are executed, which can be specific to a region or a general standard."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rule Profile indicates the set of defined rules applied to data based on different contexts, such as regional vs. common applications."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rule Profile is a conceptual framework that dictates how workflows are processed, distinguishing between regional and common rules."</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-cd1397725188cf9637d6442d0c5e89f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a18c3e1c2bbe691cf689c00b9522b1d</data>
</node>
<node id="&quot;REGIONAL VS. COMMON&quot;">
  <data key="d0">"CONCEPT"</data>
  <data key="d1">"Regional vs. Common distinguishes between different approaches or profiles that may affect how data is handled or processed."&lt;SEP&gt;"Regional vs. common refers to the distinction in workflows that may affect how rules are applied in the application, influencing the mapping process."&lt;SEP&gt;"Regional vs. Common refers to the distinction between different Rule Profiles that can be utilized based on geographical or general standards."&lt;SEP&gt;"Regional vs. common denotes the distinction between different sets of criteria or standards that can affect data validation processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Regional vs. Common distinguishes between localized and general approaches in the application of Rule Profiles in data workflows."&lt;SEP&gt;"Regional vs. common refers to different categories of Rule Profiles that could influence the mapping process."</data>
