<edge source="&quot;COMMON TABLE&quot;" target="&quot;REGIONAL TABLE&quot;">
  <data key="d3">61.0</data>
  <data key="d4">"A common table serves as a reference that may contrast with regional tables, where discrepancies can lead to mapping failures."&lt;SEP&gt;"Common Table and Regional Table represent different data levels that workflows may reference, impacting validation success."&lt;SEP&gt;"Common Tables and Regional Tables represent different levels of data mapping that can interact but may have differing values based on presentation and application requirements."&lt;SEP&gt;"Common tables may include mappings that are different from those specified in regional tables, potentially leading to mismatches in data validation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Regional Tables may include mapping values that are not present in Common Tables, indicating a difference in data availability."&lt;SEP&gt;"Regional tables may contain specific data that differs from the information in a common table, leading to potential validation issues."&lt;SEP&gt;"The existence of a mapping only in a regional table can create discrepancies when the workflow points to a common table."&lt;SEP&gt;"Regional Tables may serve as subsets or specific instances of Common Tables, catering to localized data structures.")&lt;SEP&gt;"The distinction between a common table and a regional table is critical for proper mapping and validation to avoid discrepancies in data."&lt;SEP&gt;"Regional Table and Common Table represent different levels of data structuring within financial systems, affecting the workflow integrity."</data>
